## an9.37
an9.37:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying near Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
an9.37:1.2 | S: There Ānanda addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
an9.37:1.3 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: “Friend monks!”
an9.37:1.4 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him.
an9.37:1.5 | S: Ānanda said this: | T: Ven. Ānanda said,
an9.37:2.1 | S: “It’s incredible, reverends, it’s amazing! | T: “It’s amazing, friends, it’s astounding,
an9.37:2.2 | S: How this Blessed One who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, has found an opening amid confinement. It’s in order to purify sentient beings, to get past sorrow and crying, to make an end of pain and sadness, to discover the system, and to realize extinguishment. | T: how the Blessed One who knows & sees, the worthy one, rightly self-awakened, has attained & awakened to an opening [in a confining place] for the purification of beings, for the overcoming of sorrow & lamentation, for the disappearance of pain & distress, for the attainment of the right method, & for the realization of unbinding,
an9.37:2.3 | S: The eye itself is actually present, and so are those sights. Yet one will not experience that sense-field. | T: where the eye will be, and those forms, and yet one will not be sensitive to that dimension;
an9.37:2.4 | S: The ear itself is actually present, and so are those sounds. Yet one will not experience that sense-field. | T: where the ear will be, and those sounds…
an9.37:2.5 | S: The nose itself is actually present, and so are those smells. Yet one will not experience that sense-field. | T: where the nose will be, and those aromas…
an9.37:2.6 | S: The tongue itself is actually present, and so are those tastes. Yet one will not experience that sense-field. | T: where the tongue will be, and those flavors…
an9.37:2.7 | S: The body itself is actually present, and so are those touches. Yet one will not experience that sense-field.” | T: where the body will be, and those tactile sensations, and yet one will not be sensitive to that dimension.”
an9.37:3.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Udāyī said to Venerable Ānanda: | T: When this was said, Ven. Udāyin said to Ven. Ānanda,
an9.37:3.2 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, is one who doesn’t experience that sense-field actually percipient or not?” | T: “Is one percipient when not sensitive to that dimension, my friend, or unpercipient?”
an9.37:3.3 | S: “Reverend, one who doesn’t experience that sense-field is actually percipient, not non-percipient.” | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “One is percipient when not sensitive to that dimension, my friend, not unpercipient.”
an9.37:4.1 | S: “But what does one who doesn’t experience that sense-field perceive?” | T: [Ven. Udāyin:] “When not sensitive to that dimension, my friend, one is percipient of what?”
an9.37:4.2 | S: “It’s when a mendicant, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite space. | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “There is the case where, with the complete transcending of perceptions of (physical) form, with the disappearance of perceptions of resistance, and not attending to perceptions of multiplicity, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite space,’ one enters & remains in the dimension of the infinitude of space.
an9.37:4.3 | S: One who doesn’t experience that sense-field perceives in this way. | T: Percipient in this way, one is not sensitive to that dimension [i.e, the dimensions of the five physical senses].
an9.37:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite consciousness. | T: “And further, with the complete transcending of the dimension of the infinitude of space, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite consciousness,’ one enters & remains in the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness.
an9.37:5.2 | S: One who doesn’t experience that sense-field perceives in this way. | T: Percipient in this way, too, one is not sensitive to that dimension.
an9.37:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, enters and remains in the dimension of nothingness. | T: “And further, with the complete transcending of the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness, (perceiving,) ‘There is nothing,’ one enters & remains in the dimension of nothingness.
an9.37:6.2 | S: One who doesn’t experience that sense-field perceives in this way. | T: Percipient in this way, too, one is not sensitive to that dimension.
an9.37:7.1 | S: Reverend, this one time I was staying near Sāketa in the deer park in Añjana Wood. | T: “Once, friend, when I was staying near Sāketa at the Game Refuge in the Black Forest,
an9.37:7.2 | S: Then the nun Jaṭilagāhikā came up to me, bowed, stood to one side, and said to me: | T: the nun Jaṭila Bhāgikā went to where I was staying, and on arrival—having bowed to me—stood to one side. As she was standing there, she said to me:
an9.37:7.3 | S: ‘Honorable Ānanda, regarding the immersion that does not lean forward or pull back, and is not held in place by forceful suppression. | T: ‘The concentration whereby—neither pressed down nor forced back, nor with fabrication kept blocked or suppressed—
an9.37:7.4 | S: Being free, it’s stable. Being stable, it’s content. Being content, one is not anxious. | T: still as a result of release, contented as a result of standing still, and as a result of contentment one is not agitated:
an9.37:7.5 | S: What did the Buddha say was the fruit of this immersion?’ | T: This concentration is said by the Blessed One to be the fruit of what?’
an9.37:8.1 | S: When she said this, I said to her: | T: “I said to her,
an9.37:8.2 | S: ‘Sister, regarding the immersion that does not lean forward or pull back, and is not held in place by forceful suppression. | T: ‘Sister, the concentration whereby—neither pressed down nor forced back, nor kept in place by the fabrications of forceful restraint—
an9.37:8.3 | S: Being free, it’s stable. Being stable, it’s content. Being content, one is not anxious. | T: still as a result of release, contented as a result of standing still, and as a result of contentment one is not agitated:
an9.37:8.4 | S: The Buddha said that the fruit of this immersion is enlightenment.’ | T: This concentration is said by the Blessed One to be the fruit of gnosis [arahantship].’
an9.37:8.5 | S: One who doesn’t experience that sense-field perceives in this way, too.” | T: Percipient in this way, too, one is not sensitive to that dimension.”
an9.37:8.6 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.42
an9.42:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying near Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
an9.42:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Udāyī went up to Venerable Ānanda and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Udāyin went to him and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings.
an9.42:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Ānanda, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Ānanda,
an9.42:1.4 | S: “Reverend, this was said by the godling Pañcālacaṇḍa: | T: “This has been said by Pañcālacaṇḍa the deva’s son:
an9.42:2.1 | S: ‘The opening amid confinement | T: ‘Truly in a confining place, he found an opening—
an9.42:2.2 | S: was discovered by the Buddha of vast intelligence, | T: the one of extensive wisdom,
an9.42:2.3 | S: who awakened to absorption, | T: the awakened one who awakened through jhāna,
an9.42:2.4 | S: the sage, the solitary boss bull.’ | T: the chief bull, withdrawn, the sage.’
an9.42:3.1 | S: But what is confinement, and what is the opening amid confinement that the Buddha spoke of?” | T: “Now which, my friend, is the confining place? And which opening in the confining place is the Blessed One said to have attained?”
an9.42:3.2 | S: “Reverend, these five kinds of sensual stimulation are called ‘confinement’ by the Buddha. | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “The five strings of sensuality, my friend, are described by the Blessed One as a confining place.
an9.42:3.3 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an9.42:3.4 | S: Sights known by the eye that are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: Forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire;
an9.42:3.5 | S: Sounds known by the ear … | T: sounds cognizable via the ear…
an9.42:3.6 | S: Smells known by the nose … | T: smells cognizable via the nose…
an9.42:3.7 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: tastes cognizable via the tongue…
an9.42:3.8 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
an9.42:3.9 | S: These are the five kinds of sensual stimulation that are called ‘confinement’ by the Buddha. | T: These five strings of sensuality are described by the Blessed One as a confining place.
an9.42:4.1 | S: Now, take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: “Now there is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.42:4.2 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of an opening amid confinement in a qualified sense. | T: Even this much is described by the Blessed One as the attaining of an opening in a confining place, though followed by a sequel.
an9.42:4.3 | S: But it is still confined. | T: For even there, there’s a confining place.
an9.42:4.4 | S: Confined by what? | T: What is the confining place there?
an9.42:4.5 | S: Whatever placing of the mind and keeping it connected has not ceased is the confinement there. | T: Just that directed thought & evaluation have not ceased. This is the confining place there.
an9.42:5.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled … enters and remains in the second absorption. | T: “Then there is the case where a monk, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
