## an9.44
an9.44:1.1 | S: “Reverend, they speak of one ‘freed by wisdom’. | T: [Ven. Udāyin:] “‘Released through discernment, released through discernment,’ it is said.
an9.44:1.2 | S: What is the one freed by wisdom that the Buddha spoke of?” | T: To what extent is one described by the Blessed One as released through discernment?”
an9.44:2.1 | S: “First, take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “There is the case, my friend, where a monk, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.44:2.2 | S: And they understand that with wisdom. | T: And he knows it through discernment.
an9.44:2.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the one freed by wisdom in a qualified sense. … | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released through discernment, though with a sequel. “Then, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna… the third jhāna… the fourth jhāna… the dimension of the infinitude of space… the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And he knows it through discernment. It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released through discernment, though with a sequel.
an9.44:3.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters and remains in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “Then, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, he enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling. And as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended.
an9.44:3.2 | S: And they understand that with wisdom. | T: And he knows it through discernment.
an9.44:3.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the one freed by wisdom in a definitive sense.” | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released through discernment without a sequel.”
an9.44:3.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.45
an9.45:1.1 | S: “Reverend, they speak of one ‘freed both ways’. | T: [Ven. Udāyin:] “‘Released both ways, released both ways,’ it is said.
an9.45:1.2 | S: What is the one freed both ways that the Buddha spoke of?” | T: To what extent is one described by the Blessed One as released both ways?”
an9.45:2.1 | S: “First, take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “There is the case, my friend, where a monk, secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.45:2.2 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. And they understand that with wisdom. | T: He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there, and he knows it through discernment.
an9.45:2.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the one freed both ways in a qualified sense. … | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released both ways, though with a sequel. “Then, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna… the third jhāna… the fourth jhāna… the dimension of the infinitude of space… the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there, and he knows it through discernment. It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released both ways, though with a sequel.
an9.45:3.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters and remains in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “Then, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, he enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling. And as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended.
an9.45:3.2 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. And they understand that with wisdom. | T: He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there, and he knows it through discernment.
an9.45:3.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the one freed both ways in a definitive sense.” | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as released both ways without a sequel.”
an9.45:3.4 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.62
an9.62:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, without giving up nine things you can’t realize perfection. | T: “Monks, one who hasn’t abandoned nine things is incapable of realizing arahantship.
an9.62:1.2 | S: What nine? | T: Which nine?
an9.62:1.3 | S: Greed, hate, delusion, anger, acrimony, disdain, contempt, jealousy, and stinginess. | T: Passion, aversion, delusion, anger, resentment, arrogance, insolence, envy, & stinginess.
an9.62:1.4 | S: Without giving up these nine things you can’t realize perfection. | T: One who hasn’t abandoned these nine things is incapable of realizing arahantship.
an9.62:2.1 | S: After giving up nine things you can realize perfection. | T: “One who has abandoned nine things is capable of realizing arahantship.
an9.62:2.2 | S: What nine? | T: Which nine?
an9.62:2.3 | S: Greed, hate, delusion, anger, acrimony, disdain, contempt, jealousy, and stinginess. | T: Passion, aversion, delusion, anger, resentment, arrogance, insolence, envy, & stinginess.
an9.62:2.4 | S: After giving up these nine things you can realize the fruit of perfection.” | T: One who has abandoned these nine things is capable of realizing arahantship.”
an9.62:2.5 | P: Ekādasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an9.62:2.6 | P: Khemavaggo paṭhamo. | T: (none)
an9.62:3.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an9.62:3.1 | P: Khemo ca amatañceva, | T: (none)
an9.62:3.2 | P: Abhayaṁ passaddhiyena ca; | T: (none)
an9.62:3.3 | P: Nirodho anupubbo ca, | T: (none)
an9.62:3.4 | P: Dhammaṁ pahāya bhabbena cāti. | T: (none)

## an9.63
an9.63:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five weaknesses when you’re training. | T: “Monks, these five are things that weaken the training.
an9.63:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an9.63:1.3 | S: Killing living creatures, stealing, sexual misconduct, lying, and intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: The taking of life, stealing, sexual misconduct, the telling of lies, and distilled & fermented beverages that are a cause for heedlessness.
an9.63:1.4 | S: These are the five weaknesses when you’re training. | T: These five are things that weaken the training.
an9.63:2.1 | S: To give up these five weaknesses in your training you should develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “To abandon these five things that weaken the training, one should develop the four establishings of mindfulness.
an9.63:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an9.63:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: There is the case where a monk remains focused on the body in & of itself…
an9.63:2.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in & of themselves…
an9.63:2.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind … | T: mind in & of itself…
an9.63:2.6 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
an9.63:2.7 | S: To give up those five weaknesses in your training you should develop these four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: To abandon the five things that weaken the training, one should develop these four establishings of mindfulness.”
an9.63:2.8 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.64
an9.64:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five hindrances. | T: “Monks, there are these five hindrances.
an9.64:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an9.64:1.3 | S: Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. | T: Sensual desire as a hindrance, ill will as a hindrance, sloth & drowsiness as a hindrance, restlessness & anxiety as a hindrance, and uncertainty as a hindrance.
an9.64:1.4 | S: These are the five hindrances. | T: These are the five hindrances.
an9.64:2.1 | S: To give up these five hindrances you should develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “To abandon these five hindrances, one should develop the four establishings of mindfulness.
an9.64:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an9.64:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: There is the case where a monk remains focused on the body in & of itself…
an9.64:2.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in & of themselves…
an9.64:2.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind … | T: mind in & of itself…
an9.64:2.6 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
an9.64:2.7 | S: To give up those five hindrances you should develop these four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: To abandon the five hindrances, one should develop these four establishings of mindfulness.”
an9.64:2.8 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.7
an9.7:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an9.7:1.2 | S: Then the wanderer Sutavā went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Sutavant the wanderer went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an9.7:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an9.7:2.1 | S: “Sir, this one time the Buddha was staying right here in Rājagaha, the Mountainfold. | T: “One day recently when I was staying right here in Rājagaha, at Giribbaja,
an9.7:2.2 | S: There I heard and learned this in the presence of the Buddha: | T: I heard it in the Blessed One’s presence, learned it in the Blessed One’s presence:
an9.7:2.3 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in five respects. | T: ‘Sutavant, an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these five principles.
an9.7:2.4 | S: A mendicant with defilements ended can’t deliberately take the life of a living creature, take something with the intention to steal, have sex, tell a deliberate lie, or store up goods for their own enjoyment like they used to as a lay person.’ | T: It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to intentionally deprive a living being of life. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to take, in the manner of stealing, what is not given. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to engage in sexual intercourse. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to tell a conscious lie. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to consume stored-up sensual things as he did before, when he was a householder.’
an9.7:2.5 | S: I trust I properly heard, learned, applied the mind, and remembered that from the Buddha?” | T: Now, did I hear this rightly from the Blessed One? Did I learn it rightly, attend to it rightly, understand it rightly?”
an9.7:3.1 | S: “Indeed, Sutavā, you properly heard, learned, applied the mind, and remembered that. | T: “Yes, Sutavant, you heard it rightly, learned it rightly, attended to it rightly, & understood it rightly.
an9.7:3.2 | S: In the past, as today, I say this: | T: Both before & now I say to you that
an9.7:3.3 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in nine respects. | T: an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these nine principles.
an9.7:3.4 | S: A mendicant with defilements ended can’t deliberately take the life of a living creature, take something with the intention to steal, have sex, tell a deliberate lie, or store up goods for their own enjoyment like they used to as a lay person. And they can’t make decisions prejudiced by favoritism, hostility, stupidity, or cowardice.’ | T: “[1] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to intentionally deprive a living being of life. [2] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to take, in the manner of stealing, what is not given. [3] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to engage in sexual intercourse. [4] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to tell a conscious lie. [5] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to consume stored-up sensual things as he did before, when he was a householder. “[6] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to follow a bias based on desire. [7] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to follow a bias based on aversion. [8] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to follow a bias based on fear. [9] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to follow a bias based on delusion.
an9.7:3.5 | S: In the past, as today, I say this: | T: “Both before and now I say to you that
an9.7:3.6 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in these nine respects.’” | T: an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these nine principles.”
an9.7:3.7 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.8
an9.8:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an9.8:1.2 | S: Then the wanderer Sajjha went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Sajjha the wanderer went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an9.8:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an9.8:2.1 | S: “Sir, this one time the Buddha was staying right here in Rājagaha, the Mountainfold. | T: “One day recently when I was staying right here in Rājagaha, at Giribbaja,
an9.8:2.2 | S: There I heard and learned this in the presence of the Buddha: | T: I heard it in the Blessed One’s presence, learned it in the Blessed One’s presence:
an9.8:2.3 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in five respects. | T: ‘Sajjha, an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these five principles.
an9.8:2.4 | S: A mendicant with defilements ended can’t deliberately take the life of a living creature, take something with the intention to steal, have sex, tell a deliberate lie, or store up goods for their own enjoyment like they used to as a lay person.’ | T: It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to intentionally deprive a living being of life. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to take, in the manner of stealing, what is not given. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to engage in sexual intercourse. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to tell a conscious lie. It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to consume stored-up sensual things as he did before, when he was a householder.’
an9.8:2.5 | S: I trust I properly heard, learned, applied the mind, and remembered that from the Buddha?” | T: Now, did I hear this rightly from the Blessed One? Did I learn it rightly, attend to it rightly, understand it rightly?”
an9.8:3.1 | S: “Indeed, Sajjha, you properly heard, learned, applied the mind, and remembered that. | T: “Yes, Sajjha, you heard it rightly, learned it rightly, attended to it rightly, & understood it rightly.
an9.8:3.2 | S: In the past, as today, I say this: | T: Both before & now I say to you that
an9.8:3.3 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in nine respects. | T: an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these nine principles.
an9.8:3.4 | S: A mendicant with defilements ended can’t deliberately take the life of a living creature, take something with the intention to steal, have sex, tell a deliberate lie, or store up goods for their own enjoyment like they used to as a lay person. And they can’t abandon the Buddha, the teaching, the Saṅgha, or the training.’ | T: “[1] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to intentionally deprive a living being of life. [2] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to take, in the manner of stealing, what is not given. [3] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to engage in sexual intercourse. [4] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to tell a conscious lie. [5] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to consume stored-up sensual things as he did before, when he was a householder. “[6] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to reject the Buddha. [7] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to reject the Dhamma. [8] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to reject the Saṅgha. [9] It is impossible for a monk whose effluents are ended to reject the training.
an9.8:3.5 | S: In the past, as today, I say this: | T: “Both before and now I say to you that
an9.8:3.6 | S: ‘A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—can’t transgress in these nine respects.’” | T: an arahant monk whose effluents are ended, who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, totally destroyed the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis, cannot possibly transgress these nine principles.”
an9.8:3.7 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti1
iti1:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:

