## an9.63
an9.63:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five weaknesses when you’re training. | T: “Monks, these five are things that weaken the training.
an9.63:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an9.63:1.3 | S: Killing living creatures, stealing, sexual misconduct, lying, and intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. | T: The taking of life, stealing, sexual misconduct, the telling of lies, and distilled & fermented beverages that are a cause for heedlessness.
an9.63:1.4 | S: These are the five weaknesses when you’re training. | T: These five are things that weaken the training.
an9.63:2.1 | S: To give up these five weaknesses in your training you should develop the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “To abandon these five things that weaken the training, one should develop the four establishings of mindfulness.
an9.63:2.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an9.63:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: There is the case where a monk remains focused on the body in & of itself…
an9.63:2.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: feelings in & of themselves…
an9.63:2.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind … | T: mind in & of itself…
an9.63:2.6 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
an9.63:2.7 | S: To give up those five weaknesses in your training you should develop these four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: To abandon the five things that weaken the training, one should develop these four establishings of mindfulness.”
an9.63:2.8 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

