iti1:3.1 | S: “When overcome by greed | T: The greed with which
iti1:3.2 | S: beings go to a bad place. | T: beings go to a bad destination, coveting:
iti1:3.3 | S: Having rightly understood that greed, | T: from rightly knowing that greed,
iti1:3.4 | S: the discerning give it up. | T: those who see clearly let go.
iti1:3.5 | S: Once they’ve given it up, | T: Letting go,
iti1:3.6 | S: they never return to this world.” | T: they never come to this world again.
iti1:4.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: This, too, was the meaning of what was said by the Blessed One, so I have heard.
iti1:4.2 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp1.4
snp1.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
snp1.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Magadhans in the Southern Hills near the brahmin village of Ekanāḷa. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Magadhans in Dakkhiṇāgiri near the brahman village of Ekanāḷā.
snp1.4:1.3 | S: Now at that time the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Farmer had harnessed around five hundred plows, it being the season for sowing. | T: Now at that time approximately 500 of the brahman Kasi [Plowing] Bhāradvāja’s plows were yoked at the sowing time.
snp1.4:1.4 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to where Bhāradvāja the Farmer was working. | T: Then, in the early morning, after adjusting his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe, the Blessed One went to where Kasi Bhāradvāja was working.
snp1.4:1.5 | S: Now at that time Bhāradvāja the Farmer was distributing food. | T: Now at that time Kasi Bhāradvāja’s food-distribution was underway.
snp1.4:1.6 | S: Then the Buddha went to where the distribution was taking place and stood to one side. | T: So the Blessed One went to Kasi Bhāradvāja’s food-distribution and, on arrival, stood to one side.
snp1.4:2.1 | S: Bhāradvāja the Farmer saw him standing for alms | T: Kasi Bhāradvāja saw the Blessed One standing for alms,
snp1.4:2.2 | S: and said to him, | T: and on seeing him, said to him,
snp1.4:2.3 | S: “I plough and sow, ascetic, and then I eat. | T: “I, contemplative, plow & sow. Having plowed & sown, I eat.
snp1.4:2.4 | S: You too should plough and sow, then you may eat.” | T: You, too, contemplative, should plow & sow. Having plowed & sown, you (will) eat.”
snp1.4:3.1 | S: “I too plough and sow, brahmin, and then I eat.” | T: “I, too, brahman, plow & sow. Having plowed & sown, I eat.”
snp1.4:3.2 | S: “I don’t see the worthy Gotama with a yoke or plow or plowshare or goad or oxen, yet he says: | T: “But, contemplative, we don’t see the Master Gotama’s yoke or plow, plowshare, goad, or oxen,
snp1.4:3.3 | P: Atha ca pana bhavaṁ gotamo evamāha: | T: and yet the Master Gotama says this:
snp1.4:3.4 | S: ‘I too plough and sow, brahmin, and then I eat.’” | T: ‘I, too, brahman, plow & sow. Having plowed & sown, I eat.’”
snp1.4:4.1 | S: Then Bhāradvāja the Farmer addressed the Buddha in verse: | T: Then Kasi Bhāradvāja addressed the Blessed One with a verse:
snp1.4:5.1 | S: “You claim to be a farmer, | T: You claim to be a plowman,
snp1.4:5.2 | S: but I don’t see you farming. | T: but we don’t see your plowing.
snp1.4:5.3 | S: Tell me your farming when asked, | T: Being asked, tell us about your plowing
snp1.4:5.4 | S: so I can recognize your farming.” | T: so that we may know your plowing.
snp1.4:6.1 | S: “Faith is my seed, fervor my rain, | T: The Buddha: Conviction’s my seed, austerity my rain,
snp1.4:6.2 | S: and wisdom is my yoke and plough. | T: discernment my yoke & plow,
snp1.4:6.3 | S: Conscience is my draft-pole, mind my strap, | T: shame my pole, mind my yoke-tie,
snp1.4:6.4 | S: mindfulness my plowshare and goad. | T: mindfulness my plowshare & goad.
snp1.4:7.1 | S: Guarded in body and speech, | T: Guarded in body, guarded in speech,
snp1.4:7.2 | S: I restrict my intake of food. | T: restrained in terms of belly & food,
snp1.4:7.3 | S: I use truth as my scythe, | T: I make truth a weeding-hook,
snp1.4:7.4 | S: and gentleness is my release. | T: and composure my unyoking.
snp1.4:8.1 | S: Energy is my harnessed beast, | T: Persistence, my beast of burden,
snp1.4:8.2 | S: transporting me to sanctuary from the yoke. | T: bearing me toward rest from the yoke,
snp1.4:8.3 | S: Without turning back, it goes | T: takes me, without turning back,
snp1.4:8.4 | S: to where there is no sorrow. | T: to where, having gone, one doesn’t grieve.
snp1.4:9.1 | S: That’s how to do the farming | T: That’s how my plowing is plowed.
snp1.4:9.2 | S: that has freedom from death as its fruit. | T: It has as its fruit the deathless.
snp1.4:9.3 | S: When you finish this farming | T: Having plowed this plowing,
snp1.4:9.4 | S: you’re released from all suffering.” | T: one is unyoked from all suffering & stress.
snp1.4:10.1 | S: Then Bhāradvāja the Farmer poured milk-rice in a large bronze cup and presented it to the Buddha: | T: Then Kasi Bhāradvāja, having heaped up milk-rice in a large bronze serving bowl, offered it to the Blessed One, [saying,]
snp1.4:10.2 | S: “May the worthy Gotama eat the milk-rice! | T: “May Master Gotama eat [this] milk-rice.
snp1.4:10.3 | S: The worthy one is a farmer. | T: The master is a plowman,
snp1.4:10.4 | S: For the worthy Gotama does the farming that has freedom from death as its fruit.” | T: for the Master Gotama plows the plowing that has as its fruit the deathless.”
snp1.4:11.1 | S: “Food enchanted by a verse isn’t fit for me to eat. | T: The Buddha: What’s been chanted over with verses shouldn’t be eaten by me.
snp1.4:11.2 | S: That’s not the principle of those who see, brahmin. | T: That’s not the nature, brahman, of one who’s seen rightly.
snp1.4:11.3 | S: The Buddhas cast aside things enchanted with verses. | T: What’s been chanted over with verses Awakened Ones reject.
snp1.4:11.4 | S: Since there is such a principle, brahmin, <j>that’s how they live. | T: That being their Dhamma, brahman, this is their way of life.
snp1.4:12.1 | S: Serve with other food and drink | T: Serve with other food & drink
snp1.4:12.2 | S: the consummate one, the great seer, | T: a fully-perfected great seer,
snp1.4:12.3 | S: with defilements ended and remorse stilled. | T: his effluents ended, his anxiety stilled,
snp1.4:12.4 | S: For he is the field for the seeker of merit.” | T: for that is the field for one looking for merit.
snp1.4:13.1 | S: “Then, worthy Gotama, to whom should I give the milk-rice?” | T: “Then to whom, Master Gotama, should I give this milk-rice?”
snp1.4:13.2 | S: “Brahmin, I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who can properly digest this milk-rice, except the Realized One or one of his disciples. | T: “Brahman, I don’t see that person in this world—with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk—by whom this milk-rice, having been eaten, would be rightly digested, aside from a Tathāgata or a Tathāgata’s disciple.
snp1.4:13.3 | S: Well then, brahmin, throw out the milk-rice where there is little that grows, or drop it into water that has no living creatures.” | T: In that case, brahman, throw the milk-rice away in a place without vegetation, or dump it in water with no living beings.”
snp1.4:14.1 | S: So Bhāradvāja the Farmer dropped the milk-rice in water that had no living creatures. | T: So Kasi Bhāradvāja dumped the milk-rice in water with no living beings.
snp1.4:14.2 | S: And when the milk-rice was placed in the water, it sizzled and hissed, steaming and fuming. | T: And the milk-rice, when dropped in the water, hissed & sizzled, seethed & steamed.
snp1.4:14.3 | S: Suppose there was an iron cauldron that had been heated all day. If you placed it in the water, it would sizzle and hiss, steaming and fuming. | T: Just as an iron ball heated all day, when tossed in the water, hisses & sizzles, seethes & steams,
snp1.4:14.4 | S: In the same way, when the milk-rice was placed in the water, it sizzled and hissed, steaming and fuming. | T: in the same way the milk-rice, when dropped in the water, hissed & sizzled, seethed & steamed.
snp1.4:15.1 | S: Then Bhāradvāja the Farmer, shocked and awestruck, went up to the Buddha, bowed down with his head to the Buddha’s feet, and said, | T: Then Kasi Bhāradvāja—in awe, his hair standing on end—went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, throwing himself down with his head at the Blessed One’s feet, said to him,
snp1.4:15.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent!
snp1.4:15.3 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, worthy Gotama has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
snp1.4:15.4 | S: I go for refuge to the worthy Gotama, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks. May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.
snp1.4:15.5 | S: May I receive the going forth, the ordination in the worthy Gotama’s presence?” | T: Let me obtain the Going-forth in Master Gotama’s presence, let me obtain Acceptance (into the Bhikkhu Saṅgha).”
snp1.4:16.1 | S: And Bhāradvāja the Farmer received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: Then the brahman Kasi Bhāradvāja obtained the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, he obtained Acceptance.
snp1.4:16.2 | S: Not long after his ordination, Venerable Bhāradvāja, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time reached & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, knowing & realizing it for himself in the here-&-now.
snp1.4:16.3 | S: He understood: “Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.” | T: He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
snp1.4:16.4 | S: And Venerable Bhāradvāja became one of the perfected. | T: And so Ven. Bhāradvāja became another one of the arahants.
snp1.4:16.5 | P: Kasibhāradvājasuttaṁ catutthaṁ. | T: (none)


